---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import DocPage from '../../components/docs/DocPage.astro';
import ApiTable from '../../components/docs/ApiTable.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';
import { SRC_URL, TESTS_URL } from '../../consts';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const title = 'Verdicts — DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'Why a quantum causaloid carries no verdict over its operators, and what it does instead: the Birkhoff–von Neumann projection lattice as a Verdict carrier, and Born-rule read-out to Prob at the measurement boundary.';

const born = `use deep_causality_quantum::{born_projective_prob, born_projective_probability};

// Tr(P ρ) as a real number, clamped to [0, 1].
let p: FloatType = born_projective_probability(&rho, &projection)?;

// The same number as the Prob MV-algebra verdict.
let verdict: Prob = born_projective_prob(&rho, &projection)?;`;
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/verdicts/">
  <SiteHeader slot="header" />

  <DocPage
    eyebrow="Layer · Read-out"
    title="Verdicts at the measurement boundary"
    lede="A quantum causaloid does not carry a verdict over its operators. It reads one out where a measurement happens, and the lattice it reads into is not Boolean."
    sourceHref={`${SRC_URL}/types/verdict`}
    sourceLabel="src/types/verdict"
  >
    <h2>Why there is no blanket implementation</h2>

    <p>
      The obvious move would be a <code>Verdict</code> implementation for a
      general operator or process-matrix type, so that any quantum quantity
      could be joined and met like a Boolean. The crate declines, and the reason
      is structural rather than cautious.
    </p>

    <p>
      General effects, the operators satisfying <code>0 ≤ E ≤ I</code>, form an
      effect algebra. Meet and join on an effect algebra are partial: they are
      not defined for every pair. A trait whose operations are total cannot be
      implemented faithfully on a structure whose operations are not, so
      implementing it would mean inventing values where the mathematics has
      none.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two things get implemented instead. A verdict is read out from a state and
      a measurement projection through the Born rule; and the projection itself
      is a proposition in a lattice that does have total operations.
    </p>

    <h2>The projection lattice</h2>

    <p>
      <code>Projection&lt;R, D&gt;</code> is a Hermitian idempotent on a fixed
      <code>D</code>-dimensional Hilbert space. The dimension is a const generic
      because <code>Verdict::bottom</code> and <code>Verdict::top</code> take no
      arguments and still have to know which space they inhabit.
      <code>new</code> rejects anything that is not Hermitian and idempotent to
      tolerance, so an invalid projection never exists.
    </p>

    <p>The three lattice operations are the subspace operations:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Orthocomplement.</strong> <code>P^⊥ = I − P</code>, computed
        directly.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Join.</strong> The projector onto
        <code>range(P) + range(Q)</code>, realized as the range projector of the
        positive semidefinite sum <code>P + Q</code> through its
        eigendecomposition.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Meet.</strong> <code>¬(¬P ∨ ¬Q)</code> by De Morgan, which is
        the projector onto <code>range(P) ∩ range(Q)</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The join hides a numerical decision worth stating. Deciding which
      eigenvectors belong to the range needs a cutoff, and the cutoff used is a
      numerical-rank one at roughly <code>D·ε·‖·‖</code>, not the
      <code>√ε</code> tolerance used for validation. With <code>√ε</code> the
      second eigenvector of <code>P + Q</code> is dropped whenever two distinct
      subspaces sit closer than about 1.7e-4 radians, and the join then silently
      fails to be an upper bound of its own arguments.
    </p>

    <h2>What fails, and why that is correct</h2>

    <p>
      This is an orthomodular lattice. It satisfies the bounded-lattice laws,
      the orthocomplement laws, and the orthomodular law. It does not satisfy
      distributivity, and the failure is exhibited by three projections in
      general position, such as <code>|0⟩</code>, <code>|1⟩</code> and
      <code>|+⟩</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
      That failure is the point rather than a defect, in the same way that
      <code>Prob</code> fails excluded middle and is still the right carrier for
      fuzzy verdicts. Distributivity does hold inside a commuting family, and
      <code>commutes_with</code> is how a caller finds out whether the
      propositions in hand are compatible. Both the failure and the restricted
      success are held by tests:
      <code>test_distributivity_fails_on_general_position_triple</code> and
      <code>test_distributivity_holds_within_a_commuting_family</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The LEAN statement that would tie this lattice into the core verdict
      theory is not written yet. The Rust carrier and its law tests are
      complete, and the <a href="/formalization/">formalization page</a> lists
      the gap rather than glossing it.
    </p>

    <h2>The Born read-out</h2>

    <p>
      <code>born_projective_probability</code> computes <code>Tr(Pρ)</code> and
      clamps the result to <code>[0, 1]</code>. For a Hermitian
      <code>P</code> and <code>ρ</code> the trace is real, so a non-negligible
      imaginary part means a malformed input and produces an error rather than a
      discarded component.
    </p>

    <Code code={born} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />

    <p>
      <code>born_projective_prob</code> returns the same number as
      <code>Prob</code>, the MV-algebra verdict from
      <code>deep_causality_algebra</code>. That call is the boundary: on one
      side operators and projections, on the other a classical fuzzy verdict the
      rest of the engine already knows how to combine.
    </p>

    <p>
      Both functions error with <code>DimensionMismatch</code> when the state's
      dimension and the projection's const parameter disagree, which is the one
      way the two can be mismatched given that each is validated on
      construction.
    </p>

    <p>
      The law tests for this layer live in
      <a href={`${TESTS_URL}/types/verdict`}>tests/types/verdict</a>.
    </p>
  </DocPage>

  <div class="wide-wrap">
    <ApiTable layer="verdict" title="The verdict surface" />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wide-wrap {
    max-width: var(--w-panel);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 var(--space-4) var(--space-9);
  }
  @media (min-width: 720px) { .wide-wrap { padding: 0 var(--space-5) var(--space-9); } }
</style>
